Anaemia of Chronic Disease
Haematology & Oncology
Mild to moderate fatigue/pallor, History of chronic inflammatory disease, Lack of response to oral iron, Low TIBC with normal/elevated ferritin

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-003
Reason for Visit: Acute-on-chronic functional decline and new-onset exertional dyspnoea requiring urgent evaluation to rule out acute cardiovascular compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with known chronic disease experiencing a sudden, severe worsening of fatigue and shortness of breath with minimal exertion over the past 12 hours. Urgent in-person evaluation was necessary to exclude acute myocardial ischaemia or acute haemorrhage prior to the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I've been tired for months, but since this morning I can barely walk to the bathroom without losing my breath and feeling my heart race."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a 10-year history of severe rheumatoid arthritis. They report an insidious onset of mild to moderate generalized fatigue over the past 6 months, which failed to respond to a 3-month trial of oral iron supplementation. Over the last 12 hours, the fatigue sharply escalated, compounded by new exertional dyspnoea and palpitations upon mobilizing. Appetite remains normal. No melena, haematemesis, or macroscopic blood loss noted.
Functional Status: Usually independent with a stick; currently restricted to the bed/chair due to dyspnoea.
Social/Safety Concerns: Fall risk due to sudden weakness and exertional dizziness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Pale, chronically ill-appearing adult. Mild respiratory distress upon mobilizing to the examination chair.
Physical Exam:
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, regular rhythm. Soft systolic flow murmur heard at the left sternal border. No JVP elevation.
Respiratory: Lungs clear to auscultation bilaterally. No wheeze or crackles.
Integumentary: Marked pallor of the conjunctivae, oral mucous membranes, and palmar creases.
Gastrointestinal: Abdomen soft, non-tender. No organomegaly. Digital rectal exam deferred but patient denies recent dark stools.
Musculoskeletal: Chronic rheumatoid deformities in the hands; no acute joint effusions.
Environmental Observations: House well-lit, but rugs present a trip hazard for a weakened patient.
Medication Reconciliation: Methotrexate and Prednisolone confirmed. Over-the-counter iron supplements ceased last week due to lack of efficacy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Anaemia of Chronic Disease - Status: Exacerbated (Symptomatic)
Differentials:

Acute Gastrointestinal Bleed - Ruled out/unlikely due to: normal blood pressure, absence of melena/haematemesis, and stable abdomen.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: absence of chest pain, diaphoresis, or radiation, with symptoms strictly tied to exertion and anaemia.

Iron Deficiency Anaemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: documented lack of response to empiric oral iron therapy and underlying chronic inflammatory state.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits significant acute-on-chronic functional deterioration driven by severe symptomatic anaemia in the context of known rheumatoid arthritis. The new onset of exertional dyspnoea and compensatory resting tachycardia indicates the cardiovascular system is struggling to meet oxygen demands. Delaying assessment until normal hours would risk unrecognized myocardial ischaemia or a syncopal fall resulting in trauma. The urgent after-hours visit was essential to clinically exclude active acute haemorrhage, assess haemodynamic stability, and establish a safe management plan for impending laboratory evaluation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Strict bed rest overnight. Urgent outpatient pathology tomorrow morning for FBC, iron studies (anticipating low TIBC, normal/elevated ferritin, low serum iron), and CRP.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Transition from lying to sitting slowly, pausing for 30 seconds before standing. By allowing your blood pressure time to adjust, you prevent sudden drops in brain oxygenation and reduce fall risk.

Place a chair in the bathroom and at the sink. By conserving energy during essential daily tasks, you reduce the workload on your heart and prevent sudden breathlessness.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to community nursing for in-home pathology collection tomorrow.
DME/Safety (Optional): Shower chair recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours to discuss pathology results and potential intravenous iron or erythropoietin-stimulating agents.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 immediately if you develop chest pain, severe shortness of breath at rest, fainting, or if you vomit blood or pass black, tarry stools overnight.

